## Development style

Default to this workflow:

```text
Inspect -> Plan -> Implement one small task -> Validate -> Stop and report
```

Do not continue into the next feature unless the user asks.

A good task is one of these:

- one template part
- one CSS component
- one ACF field group plan
- one CPT/taxonomy registration file
- one page template
- one archive template
- one product single section
- one JS interaction
- one bug fix
- one responsive fix
- one accessibility improvement

A bad task is:

- "build the whole site"
- "implement all product pages and homepage"
- "rewrite the theme"
- "refactor all CSS"
- "create all templates at once"
- "connect Figma and build everything"
- "change header, footer, product pages, homepage, CPTs, and CSS in one pass"

## Maximum change size

Unless the user explicitly asks for a larger implementation:

- edit at most 3 files per task
- create at most 2 new files per task
- implement at most 1 feature/component/template per task
- avoid touching unrelated files
- avoid broad formatting-only diffs
- avoid rewriting a whole file when a patch is enough

If more files are truly required, explain why before editing.

## Before editing code

Before any meaningful code edit, state:

```text
Task:
Relevant rule files:
Files to inspect:
Files to edit:
What I will not touch:
Commerce/auth elements excluded:
Validation steps:
```

Keep it short.

Example:

```text
Task: Implement product card component.
Relevant rule files: main, project context, Figma MCP, WordPress skill.
Files to inspect: existing product archive, CSS, helper files.
Files to edit: template-parts/components/product-card.php, assets/css/main.css.
What I will not touch: CPT registration, header, footer, product single page.
Commerce/auth elements excluded: price, buy button, cart, wishlist, account.
Validation steps: check escaping, image rendering, responsive card layout.
```

## After editing code

After each task, report:

```text
Changed files:
What was implemented:
What was intentionally not implemented:
How to test:
Suggested next small task:
```

Do not start the suggested next task automatically.

## Figma-driven task workflow

When using Figma MCP:

1. Use the smallest useful selected frame/component.
2. Describe the design in plain terms.
3. Map it to WordPress:
   - template
   - template part
   - ACF fields
   - CPT data
   - CSS block
   - JS only if needed
4. Identify commerce/auth elements to remove.
5. Implement only that selected component/section.
6. Stop.

Do not select a full page if the current task is one component.

Do not generate static HTML copies of Figma pages. Build reusable WordPress components.

## Recommended implementation phases

Follow this project sequence unless the user changes priorities.

### Phase 0: Project setup

Goal:

- clean repo structure
- Cursor rules installed
- theme skeleton exists
- local WordPress runs

Small tasks:

```text
0.1 Add/check theme header and required files.
0.2 Add functions.php loader.
0.3 Add inc/setup.php.
0.4 Add inc/assets.php.
0.5 Add assets/css/main.css foundation.
0.6 Add basic header/footer shell.
```

Stop after each task.

### Phase 1: Data model

Goal:

- define structured content before complex templates

Small tasks:

```text
1.1 Add selecta_product CPT only.
1.2 Add product taxonomies.
