## How to handle existing code

Before changing existing code:

- read the relevant files
- identify the current pattern
- preserve naming conventions
- patch only the needed area
- do not reformat unrelated code
- do not replace working code wholesale
- do not remove code unless clearly unused or harmful
- when uncertain, add a small TODO instead of large speculative changes

## Anti-overwrite rules

Never do these without explicit user approval:

- replace the entire theme
- rewrite all CSS
- replace all templates
- rename CPTs/taxonomies after content exists
- delete existing ACF field groups
- restructure folders broadly
- introduce a framework
- introduce WooCommerce
- add public auth/account systems
- convert plain CSS to Sass/Tailwind/Bootstrap
- create a page-builder architecture
- change public URL slugs after they are agreed

## Branch and commit guidance

If Git is available:

- use a feature branch per task or small group of related tasks
- keep commits small
- use commit messages that describe the user-facing change
- do not mix refactoring and feature work in the same commit
- inspect diffs before finalizing

Example commits:

```text
feat(product): add catalog product card component
feat(product): add product archive template
style(header): implement mobile nav layout
fix(acf): guard product gallery when field is empty
```

## Validation checklist for every task

Before reporting done:

- no PHP syntax issues introduced
- no obvious fatal if ACF is inactive
- dynamic output is escaped
- images use WordPress image functions where possible
- CSS is scoped to the component
- responsive behavior is considered
- keyboard/focus states are considered for interactive UI
- no commerce UI introduced
- no public auth UI introduced
- changed files match the task scope
- no unrelated large rewrite happened

## Agent behavior when scope grows

If the user asks for a large feature:

1. Break it into small tasks.
2. Show the first 3-6 tasks.
3. Implement only the first task unless the user asks for more.
4. Stop after completing the task.

Example:

```text
Large request: Build the full product detail page.

Breakdown:
1. Product detail hero/gallery.
2. Product overview/meta fields.
3. Benefits/results section.
4. Ingredients section.
5. Usage instructions section.
6. Related products/advice.

Implement now: task 1 only.
```

## Agent behavior when Figma differs from scope

If Figma includes forbidden commerce/auth UI:

- mention it briefly
- do not implement it
- replace with neutral catalog UI only if needed
- do not ask repeatedly unless the design cannot work without a decision

Example:

```text
The Figma frame includes a cart icon and price area. I will not implement those because the project is catalog-only. I will keep the product title, image, range, short description, and detail-page link.
```

## Definition of done for the project

The project is ready when:

- core templates exist
- product CPT and taxonomies work
- product archive and single pages are catalog-only
- homepage matches the approved Figma design
- global header/mega menu/mobile menu/footer work
- advice/CMS/legal/contact/partners pages are implemented as needed
- editable content is managed through WordPress admin/ACF
- no e-commerce or public auth exists
- responsive layout is acceptable
- accessibility basics are covered
- SEO basics are covered
- tracking/forms/GDPR are handled according to final plugin/config decisions
- unused code is removed
- the final diff is understandable

## Final reminder

Small tasks are not optional. They are the main safety mechanism for AI-assisted development on this project.

When in doubt, reduce scope.
